<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- =====================================================================================
     ecr88_dynamics.xacro  -  mass / CoM / inertia / joint limits for the Volvo ECR88
     =====================================================================================
     AUDIT DOCUMENT (read it before trusting any number here):
         resources/ECR88_estimated_dynamics.md
     Every property below is traceable to a numbered row of that document's section 6
     confidence ledger. The ledger row number is quoted in each comment as [L<nn>].

     TARGET MACHINE, ASSERTED and NOT YET CONFIRMED (see H4):
         Volvo ECR88D, Tier 4f / Stage V (open centre, negative flow, 2x68+54 l/min)
         mono boom 3.55 m, arm 1.7 m, rubber track 450 mm, standard counterweight 1 400 kg
         published operating weight 8 752 kg
     There are FOUR machines called "ECR88" and their masses, swing torque, swing speed and
     counterweights all differ. See section 0 of the audit document. In particular the
     22.9 kN.m swing torque and the 1 400 kg counterweight are ECR88D figures and must NOT
     be relabelled onto an ECR88 Plus.

     CONFIDENCE TAGS (the project's single rule, CLAUDE.md):
         PUBLISHED     manufacturer figure, URL in the audit document
         OEM-TEAM      supplied by Xpanner staff with the real machine; outranks PUBLISHED
         DERIVED       computed, derivation shown in the audit document
         CLASS-TYPICAL industry norm for this machine class, no ECR88 specific source
         GUESS         placeholder
     A guess labelled as a guess is fine. A guess presented as published is not.

     ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
     FIVE INTEGRATION HAZARDS. Read before including this file.
     ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
     H1  [RESOLVED 2026-09-11] NAME COLLISION. This file used to define the same property
         names as sections [14][C] and
         [15][C]/[E] of ecr88_params.xacro. Load exactly ONE of the two: xacro silently
         keeps the LAST definition, so loading both produces a working file with values
         nobody chose. Intended migration: delete those sections from ecr88_params.xacro
         and include this file instead, leaving that file purely geometric (which is what
         its own header says it wants to be).
         DONE: the 48 duplicated properties and 16 dead legacy mass_* aliases were removed
         from ecr88_params.xacro and this file is now included from ecr88.urdf.xacro.
         Overlap between the two files is zero; nothing depends on last-definition-wins.
     H2  [RESOLVED 2026-09-11] TRACK DOUBLE COUNT. m_base here is the WHOLE undercarriage including both tracks
         (2 713 kg), which is the decomposition the audit document's mass table uses.
         ecr88.urdf.xacro currently gives base_link m_base AND gives each of the two track
         links m_track. base_link NOW POINTS AT m_base_frame (2 319 kg); before that fix the
         machine carried 394 kg of phantom track and totalled 9 543 kg against a published
         8 752. It now totals 9 149.1 kg, matching the audit document exactly.
     H3  [RESOLVED 2026-09-11] ARM JOINT SIGN. ecr88_params.xacro used to emit lim_arm_lower/upper as
         -149 deg / -25 deg. With axis (0,1,0), origin rpy 0 0 0 and arm_link built along
         +X, a POSITIVE rotation takes the arm tip DOWN, which is arm IN. Curling in is
         therefore positive and those limits are sign inverted. Verified by FK: at the
         team's boom ground anchor, arm = -149 deg puts the bucket pin 1.9 m ABOVE ground
         with the arm folded up over the boom, while +149 deg gives the real tucked
         transport posture 0.17 m above ground. The values below use the corrected sign.
         Section 5.3 of the audit document has the full working.
         The values below use the corrected sign, and Olivia's own labels ("31 arm-out,
         155 arm-in", 2026-09-11) independently confirm that the angle grows as the arm
         curls in, which is this file's positive direction. The sign is no longer inferred.
     H4  EMISSION STAGE IS NOT CONFIRMED. The Stage V pick above is an ASSERTION. The audit
         document's only discriminator was WITHDRAWN 2026-09-11: it compared the surveyed
         house box LenUpX = 2.28 m against Volvo's published "overall width of upper
         structure", but X is the FORE-AFT axis in this frame (proved by LenBottomX 2.820
         vs published track LENGTH 2.830, and LenBottomY 2.320 vs gauge+shoe 2.300). The
         surveyed WIDTH is LenUpY = 2.46 m, which misses Stage V 2 260 mm by +8.85 % and
         Stage IIIB 2 210 mm by +11.31 %: it separates nothing. Blocked on the serial plate
         and NOT usable as evidence:
             m_published_operating_weight 8 752   (Stage IIIB reads 8 810 kg, CW 1 480)
             m_counterweight              1 400   (Stage IIIB 1 480)
             m_base, m_house and therefore EVERY mass here, which scale off 8 752
             vel_swing 0.869 rad/s                (Stage IIIB 0.974 rad/s = 9.3 r/min)
             the 136 l/min flow assumption behind vel_boom / vel_arm / vel_bucket
                                                  (Stage IIIB is 1 x 169 l/min closed centre LS)
             aux_rotator_flow 28 l/min            (Stage IIIB 35 l/min: see section [7])
         Separately, LenUpY 2.46 vs the published 2.26 m is a 200 mm OPEN DISCREPANCY. Its
         only consumer is house_link: if 2.26 is right, inr_house_ixx falls 2 628.40 ->
         2 347.87 and inr_house_izz 5 339.23 -> 5 058.71. The survey value is kept.
         Audit document section 0 and ledger row 34.
     H5  THE ARM OUT STOP IS AN ASSUMPTION, NOT A DERIVATION. lim_arm_lower = 0 (arm
         collinear with the boom) was previously justified by a residual against Volvo's
         H "min front swing radius" 2 560 mm. That check was WITHDRAWN 2026-09-11: it
         assumed the arm and tool fold inside 2.5834 m, and FK on this file's own values
         shows the bucket pin never comes inside 3.370 m and the teeth never inside
         3.731 m anywhere in the workspace. The agreement was coincidental. The other two
         published figures cannot vote either (max reach A defines R_eff by construction;
         reach on ground B contains neither L3 nor the arm angle). What remains is the
         standard layout plus ref_arm_deadcentre at -5.6 deg. Consequences: lim_arm_lower,
         ref_bucket_radius and lim_boom_mech_upper all inherit this. Teo's cylinder
         strokes close it. Audit document section 5.2 steps 4 and 6, ledger rows 39/41/44.
     ===================================================================================== -->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!-- ============================================================================
       [0]  ANGLE HELPER
       ============================================================================ -->
  <xacro:property name="deg" value="${pi/180}"/>   <!-- EXACT. Degrees to radians. Every limit below is written as deg*<degrees> so it stays auditable against the document. -->

  <!-- ============================================================================
       [1]  MASS  [kg]
       Anchor: published operating weight 8 752 kg. The budget closes exactly:
         2713 base + 4966 house + 530 boom + 280 arm assy + 188 bucket + 75 operator = 8752
       As built (tiltrotator and suction head instead of the 188 kg bucket, no operator)
       the same links sum to 9 149 kg. See audit document section 3.3.
       ============================================================================ -->
  <xacro:property name="m_base"        value="2713.0"/>  <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL [L8]  undercarriage INCLUDING both tracks, blade, final drives = 0.31 x 8752 kg operating weight. 0.31 is the middle of the published 28-34 % class band, nudged low because this reduced tail swing design loads the house. See H2. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_house"       value="${4966.0 - m_cyl_boom_total}"/>  <!-- DERIVED (RESIDUAL) [L9]  8752 minus base, boom, arm assy, bucket, operator, then minus the boom cylinder now modelled as its own links (it was inside this residual). Every error in m_base lands here one for one: this is the largest single uncertainty in the model. Includes the 1 400 kg published counterweight. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_boom"        value="${530.0 - m_cyl_arm_total}"/>   <!-- PUBLISHED [L2]  Volvo component table, 3.55 m mono boom, "includes cylinder, piping and pin, excludes boom cyl. pin". The dipper arm cylinder Volvo counts in that 530 kg is now its own pair of links, so it is carved out here rather than counted twice. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_arm"         value="301.0"/>   <!-- DERIVED from PUBLISHED [L10]  2.1 m dipper. Published 340 kg arm assembly ("includes cylinder, linkage and pin") minus the carved out 4 bar: 340 - 22 - 16 - 1 = 301. The bucket cylinder stays inside this figure. For the 1.7 m arm the published assembly is 280 kg and this becomes 241. Volvo publishes both rows; Teo highlighted 2.1 m as the fleet spec (2026-09-11). -->
  <xacro:property name="m_inp_link"    value="22.0"/>    <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL [L11]  power link, pair of 0.42 m plates approx 20 x 140 mm section plus bosses. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_conn_rod"    value="16.0"/>    <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL [L12]  0.40 m idler link, lighter section than the power link. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_outp_link"   value="35.0"/>    <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL [L13]  quick coupler / tiltrotator top hitch about the bucket pin. NOT part of Volvo's 280 kg: on a bucket machine this member is the bucket's own top lug, so it is tool side. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_gnd_link"    value="1.0"/>     <!-- GUESS, TOKEN [L14]  the ground link is NOT a physical part: it is the arm segment from the input link pin to the bucket pin (1.7 - 1.441 = 0.259 = lenGndLink). Its real mass is already inside m_arm. Token so PhysX never sees a zero mass body on a live joint. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_tilt_mount"  value="45.0"/>    <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL, NO SOURCE [L15]  tiltrotator hitch body above the tilt axis. The research found NOTHING on the Xpanner X1 PanelLift. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_tilt"        value="110.0"/>   <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL, NO SOURCE [L16]  tilt housing plus tilt cylinders. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_rotator"     value="145.0"/>   <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL, NO SOURCE [L17]  rotator motor, worm gear and slew bearing: the heaviest single part of the tiltrotator. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_attachment"  value="120.0"/>   <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL, NO SOURCE [L18]  suction adapter / frame. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_probe"       value="25.0"/>    <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL, NO SOURCE [L19]  probe stand off member spanning distProbeToContactSurface. -->

  <!-- Needed by ecr88.urdf.xacro's property contract but not part of the audit
       document's 13 link table. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_contact_surface" value="180.0"/> <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL, NO SOURCE [L20]  suction plate assembly, the TCP link. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_track"       value="197.0"/>   <!-- DERIVED from PUBLISHED weight deltas [L21]  PER TRACK, rubber 450 mm. Volvo publishes the same machine with steel 450 (8 939 kg) and rubber 450 (8 752 kg): 187 kg for the pair, 93.5 kg per side. With a class typical steel chain of 290 kg/side (39 links at 154 mm pitch) the rubber track is 196.5 kg/side. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_base_frame"  value="${m_base - 2*m_track}"/> <!-- DERIVED  = 2 319 kg. USE THIS for base_link when the two track links are emitted separately, so the tracks are not counted twice. See H2. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_marker"      value="0.01"/>    <!-- GUESS, TOKEN  coordinate frame markers (boom_mount, gnss x2, cyl anchors x3). Small enough to be dynamically irrelevant, large enough that no importer trips over it. -->

  <!-- Totals, COMPUTED so they cannot go stale. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_total_asbuilt"
                  value="${m_base + m_house + m_boom + m_arm + m_gnd_link + m_inp_link
                          + m_conn_rod + m_outp_link + m_tilt_mount + m_tilt + m_rotator
                          + m_attachment + m_probe + m_contact_surface}"/>
                  <!-- DERIVED, COMPUTED so it can never go stale: sum of the 14 link masses above = 9 149 kg. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_published_operating_weight" value="8752.0"/>      <!-- PUBLISHED [L1]  ECR88D Stage V, rubber 450, CW 1 400, arm 1.7, bucket 188. The as built total exceeds it by 397 kg = tool stack 660 - bucket 188 - operator 75. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_counterweight"              value="1400.0"/>      <!-- PUBLISHED [L4]  inside m_house. Stage V standard; heavy option 1 610, Stage IIIB 1 480. -->

  <!-- ============================================================================
       [2]  JOINT LIMITS  [rad]
       Convention, stated once and obeyed everywhere below (audit document 5.1):
         REP-103, X forward / Y left / Z up.
         boom, arm and bucket joints all use axis (0,1,0), so a POSITIVE URDF angle
         rotates the child's +X toward -Z: THE LINK TIP GOES DOWN.
           boom_joint   zero = boom pin to pin axis horizontal forward; + = boom DOWN
           arm_joint    zero = arm collinear with the boom (fully out);  + = arm curls IN
           bucket_joint zero = output_link +X collinear with arm +X;     + = tool curls IN
           tilt_joint   axis (1,0,0);  rotator_joint axis (0,0,1), continuous
       ============================================================================ -->

  <!-- SWING. The real house slews endlessly; change the joint type to `continuous`
       and drop these two if endless slew is wanted. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_swing_lower"  value="${-pi}"/>            <!-- PUBLISHED behaviour [L51]  machine slews 360 deg endlessly. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_swing_upper"  value="${ pi}"/>            <!-- PUBLISHED behaviour [L51] -->

  <!-- BOOM, PRIMARY = X1 PanelLift OPERATING envelope, not the mechanical stops.
       OEM-TEAM span 38 deg (Olivia, 2026-09-11: "-31 boom down ground reference to -69
       max") placed by the project's bisection onto the boom angle at which the TCP first
       touches ground (z = lenBottomZ1). Kept unchanged from ecr88_params.xacro so the
       team's FK verification still holds. -->
  <!-- These used to hardcode the RESULT of the anchor solve that ecr88_params.xacro
       performs (deg * -26.47 and deg * 11.53). That made the boom anchor a two-source
       value: switching machine_variant re-solved bm1GroundContactUrdf in params and
       these literals silently kept the old 1.7 m placement, so the boom limits did not
       move with the arm. Derive them from the params properties instead, so the anchor
       lives in exactly one place.
         jntBm1Lower/Upper in params compute the same thing; they are the same two
         numbers by construction and are now unused aliases. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_boom_lower"   value="${deg * (bm1GroundContactUrdf - oemBm1AngleSpan)}"/>
                                      <!-- OEM-TEAM span + DERIVED placement [L42]  boom fully UP. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_boom_upper"   value="${deg * bm1GroundContactUrdf}"/>
                                      <!-- OEM-TEAM span + DERIVED placement [L42]  boom DOWN, TCP at ground. -->

  <!-- BOOM, MECHANICAL envelope. Needed for trenching; the PanelLift range above cannot
       dig at all (38 deg of boom never reaches below ground, and the published 4 130 mm
       digging depth needs 123 deg). DERIVED from Volvo's working range table:
         upper: F max cutting height 6 790 = h + R_eff*sin(b_hi), R_eff = A - r0 = 6.030 m
                -> +62.42 deg machine frame.
         lower: C max digging depth 4 130 = h + L1*sin(b_lo) - (L2+L3) -> -60.67 deg.
       NEITHER IS A CHECKED VALUE. Each solves one unknown from one published figure, so
       there is no residual and nothing can fail. The former cross check against H (min
       front swing radius 2 560 mm) is WITHDRAWN: see H5. The UPPER stop additionally
       inherits H5's arm out stop assumption, because b_hi is solved with the chain
       straight: +62.42 deg at arm out stop 0, +66.56 deg at -10, +70.81 deg at -20.
       The one surviving independent test in this derivation is on the boom FOOT, not on
       these stops: B reach on ground 6 800 = r0 + sqrt(R_eff^2 - h^2) closes to -5.7 mm
       and pins h = 1.445 +/- 0.023 m.
       Machine frame angles are + up; URDF is the negative of that. The PanelLift range
       above sits entirely inside this one, as it must. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_boom_mech_lower" value="${deg * -62.42}"/> <!-- DERIVED [L41], INHERITS the H5 arm out stop ASSUMPTION  boom fully UP, from published max cutting height 6 790 mm with the chain assumed straight. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_boom_mech_upper" value="${deg *  60.67}"/> <!-- DERIVED [L41]  boom fully DOWN, from published max digging depth 4 130 mm. -->

  <!-- ARM, PRIMARY = OEM-TEAM SPAN with DERIVED PLACEMENT. Same method CLAUDE.md already
       blessed for the boom. Olivia's span of 124 deg is excellent and independently
       matches the arm cylinder's stroke feasible sweep (123.3 deg at a retracted/stroke
       ratio of 1.60). Her PLACEMENT (interior angle 31..155 deg) is contradicted twice:
         (a) it needs 936 mm of stroke on a 1 366 mm retracted length, ratio 1.46, below
             what a hydraulic cylinder can physically be built to;
         (b) published max reach A and reach on ground B both close on FULL collinear
             extension to within 6 mm, which requires the arm to reach interior 180 deg.
       (b) IS WITHDRAWN AS CIRCULAR: max reach A defines R_eff = A - r0 and the bucket
           radius is then back solved against it, so A closes for ANY arm out stop, and
           reach on ground B = r0 + sqrt(R_eff^2 - h^2) contains neither. Only (a), the
           stroke ratio, still argues against Olivia's literal placement.
       So the span is kept and the out stop is ASSUMED at collinear, on the standard
       layout plus ref_arm_deadcentre. NOTE H3 on the sign and H5 on the tag. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_arm_lower"    value="${deg *   0.00}"/>   <!-- ASSUMPTION [L44], was DERIVED until the H residual was withdrawn (H5)  arm fully OUT, collinear with the boom. NO published figure discriminates this placement. Standing support: it is the standard layout, and the arm cylinder is within 5.6 deg of dead centre here (moment arm 72 mm), so a STRUCTURAL stop belongs about there: prefer a SOFT joint limit. If the true out stop is -10 deg instead, ref_bucket_radius becomes 0.802 and lim_boom_mech_lower becomes -66.56 deg. Teo's cylinder strokes settle it. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_arm_upper"    value="${deg * 124.00}"/>   <!-- OEM-TEAM span [L43]  arm fully IN (interior angle 56 deg). -->

  <!-- 2026-09-11 UPDATE, Olivia (Slack #simulation, 11:28 KST). The original relay of her
       figures carried no direction words; the full message does:
           "암 : 31(암아웃) ~ 155(암인)"
       So the angle GROWS as the arm curls IN. That is the same sense as this URDF
       (positive arm_joint = arm in, established by FK in H3), which independently
       CONFIRMS the sign correction — it is no longer an inference.
       It also supports the placement below: 31 = arm fully OUT maps onto lim_arm_lower,
       and lim_arm_lower is set at the collinear out stop, giving 0..124 with a constant
       -31 deg offset between machine and URDF frames. What Olivia's numbers still do NOT
       settle is whether her 31 is measured at the SAME posture as our collinear zero;
       Teo's cylinder strokes remain the thing that decides it. -->

  <!-- ARM, literal OEM-TEAM placement, sign corrected per H3. One edit away if Olivia
       confirms the interior angle reading. Cylinder infeasible as noted above. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_arm_oem_lower" value="${deg *  25.00}"/>  <!-- OEM-TEAM literal [L45]  interior angle 155 deg. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_arm_oem_upper" value="${deg * 149.00}"/>  <!-- OEM-TEAM literal [L45]  interior angle 31 deg. -->

  <!-- BUCKET (the tool carrier about the bucket pin).
       SUPERSEDED 2026-09-11 by Olivia (Slack #simulation, 11:28 KST), quoted verbatim:
           "ECR88 기준입니다 ... 버킷 : -126(버킷아웃) ~ 43(버킷인)"
       That is the REAL machine as built, with the tiltrotator and PanelLift head fitted.
       It replaces the previous PUBLISHED-span + CLASS-TYPICAL-placement pair, which read
       -100..+90 from Volvo's published 190 deg bucket rotation. Span drops 190 -> 169 deg:
       the published 190 belongs to a bucket on its own lugs, not to this tool stack.

       SIGN: checked by FK before adopting, not assumed. Holding boom +11.53, arm +60 and
       sweeping bucket_joint, the TCP measured from the bucket pin goes
           bucket = -126  ->  (+1.488, -0.206)  reaching forward   = 버킷아웃
           bucket =  +43  ->  (-1.500, -0.081)  folded back        = 버킷인
       so negative = out, positive = in, which is Olivia's convention exactly. The numbers
       transfer with NO offset and NO sign flip. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_bucket_lower" value="${deg * -126.00}"/>  <!-- OEM-TEAM [L47]  버킷아웃, fully dumped / open. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_bucket_upper" value="${deg *   43.00}"/>  <!-- OEM-TEAM [L47]  버킷인, fully curled. Span 169 deg. -->

  <!-- TILT.
       SUPERSEDED 2026-09-11 by Olivia (same message): "틸트 : -45~45".
       The previous value was a CLASS-TYPICAL +/-40 with no source at all; the note there
       already observed that Steelwrist units run +/-45, and the real machine is +/-45. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_tilt_lower"   value="${deg * -45.00}"/>   <!-- OEM-TEAM [L49] -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_tilt_upper"   value="${deg *  45.00}"/>   <!-- OEM-TEAM [L49] -->

  <!-- ROTATOR stays CONTINUOUS: no lim_rotator_* exists on purpose. Olivia's
       "로테이트 : -180 ~ 180" is the full circle, i.e. it does NOT constrain the joint.
       A continuous joint already covers it, and modelling it as revolute with +/-pi stops
       would invent a hard stop at the wrap point that the real rotator does not have.
       The survey corroborates the axis, distRotToAtt being purely -Z (0, 0, -0.2615). [L50] -->

  <!-- Passive 4 bar dressing branch. The URDF cut is at the conn rod / output link pin,
       so this branch is VISUAL ONLY and carries no load. Wide placeholders. -->
  <!-- input_link_joint CARRIES A MIMIC ON bucket_joint. A mimic says the two joints
       are the same degree of freedom up to multiplier and offset, so their LIMITS must
       be the same interval mapped through that same relation. These used to be a
       free-hand GUESS of [-170, -20] against a bucket range of [-126, +43], i.e. the
       mimic demanded angles the limit forbade over most of the bucket's travel.

       That is not a cosmetic mismatch. Two hard constraints pulling against each other
       inject large forces into the chain, and those forces do not stay in the dressing
       branch: they loaded the whole front end. Measured at the rest pose, with the
       4-bar present the machine settled 11.8 / 14.0 / 36.2 deg away from its commanded
       boom / arm / bucket angles; with the 4-bar dropped, the same pose held to
       0.33 / 0.07 / 0.02 deg. That drift was read as "the drives are too weak" and then
       as "the cylinders are exploding", and it was neither. Analytic gravity torque
       at this pose is 36.8 kN.m on a boom with 121.3 kN.m of effort, a 3.3x margin.

       With the placeholder multiplier 1.0 / offset 0.0 the mapping is the identity, so
       the limits are simply the bucket's. WHEN THE REAL 4-BAR COEFFICIENTS ARE FITTED,
       THESE MUST BE MAPPED THROUGH THEM TOO, or the same fight comes back. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_inp_link_lower" value="${bkt_mimic_mult * lim_bucket_lower + bkt_mimic_off}"/>
                                         <!-- DERIVED from lim_bucket_* through the mimic -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_inp_link_upper" value="${bkt_mimic_mult * lim_bucket_upper + bkt_mimic_off}"/>
                                         <!-- DERIVED from lim_bucket_* through the mimic -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_conn_rod_lower" value="${-pi}"/>           <!-- GUESS  the cut member: free by construction. -->
  <xacro:property name="lim_conn_rod_upper" value="${ pi}"/>           <!-- GUESS  the cut member: free by construction. -->

  <!-- ============================================================================
       [3]  EFFORT  [N.m]
       ============================================================================ -->
  <!-- ==================================================================
       CYLINDER BORE AND ROD, 2026-09-11.  These were listed under "searched for
       and NOT found" until now; they are not in any Volvo brochure or product
       guide, but the AFTERMARKET SEAL-KIT catalogues index them by machine and
       publish bore and rod for every cylinder on the machine:

         cylinder | rod   | bore   | seal kit  | cylinder P/N
         boom     |  70mm | 105mm  | 14589167  | 14528551 / 14528552
         arm      |  60mm |  90mm  | 14589168  |
         bucket   |  55mm |  80mm  | 14589169  | 14522092 / 14522093
         blade    |  95mm | 130mm  | 14589165  |

       Sources: oringsandmore.com/volvo-seal-kits (all four),
       hwpartstore.com Volvo ECR88 boom / bucket seal kit pages (bore, rod and
       cylinder part numbers, serial 10001-Up).
       Tag: PUBLISHED, but by a parts vendor rather than by Volvo. That is a weaker
       provenance than the product guide and it is why the tag is annotated.

       THIS VALIDATED TWO EARLIER DERIVATIONS AND BROKE ONE:
         eff_boom assumed a 105 mm bore as CLASS-TYPICAL  -> actual 105 mm. Exact.
         eff_arm back-solved to an 89.8 mm bore from the published tearout force
                                                         -> actual 90 mm. Exact.
         eff_inp_link assumed an 85 mm bucket bore        -> actual 80 mm. WRONG,
                 corrected below. It was 11 % too high.
       Two independent routes landing on the exact catalogue bore is the strongest
       corroboration this model has had; the bucket miss is the reminder that a
       CLASS-TYPICAL tag is a real admission, not a formality.

       Cylinder forces at the published 29.4 MPa implement relief:
         boom   extend 254.6 kN   retract 141.4 kN
         arm    extend 187.0 kN   retract 103.9 kN
         bucket extend 147.8 kN   retract  77.9 kN
         blade  extend 390.2 kN   retract 181.8 kN
       ================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_boom_bore"   value="0.105"/>  <!-- PUBLISHED (parts catalogue) [m] -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_boom_rod"    value="0.070"/>  <!-- PUBLISHED (parts catalogue) [m] -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_arm_bore"    value="0.090"/>  <!-- PUBLISHED (parts catalogue) [m] -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_arm_rod"     value="0.060"/>  <!-- PUBLISHED (parts catalogue) [m] -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_bkt_bore"    value="0.080"/>  <!-- PUBLISHED (parts catalogue) [m] -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_bkt_rod"     value="0.055"/>  <!-- PUBLISHED (parts catalogue) [m] -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_blade_bore"  value="0.130"/>  <!-- PUBLISHED (parts catalogue) [m] -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_blade_rod"   value="0.095"/>  <!-- PUBLISHED (parts catalogue) [m] -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_relief_pa"   value="29400000.0"/> <!-- PUBLISHED  implement relief 29.4 MPa, product guide -->
  <!-- STROKE IS STILL NOT FOUND. Seal-kit catalogues never carry it. Without it the
       cylinder-length to joint-angle map stays open, and so does the arm out-stop
       placement. It is now the single most valuable missing number in this file. -->

  <!-- ==================================================================
       VISIBLE HYDRAULIC CYLINDERS.  Cosmetic dressing, mimic-slaved.

       A cylinder spans a fixed pin on the parent link and a moving pin on the
       child link, so its inclination and its extension are both NON-LINEAR
       functions of the joint angle, and URDF <mimic> is linear only. The
       question was therefore not "can we" but "how wrong is the straight line".
       Measured over each joint's actual range, with a MINIMAX (not least
       squares) fit, which is what you want when the cap on the error is the
       thing that matters:

         cylinder | pitch err | rod-tip err | vs the 7.2 m machine
         boom     |  0.23 deg |    16.8 mm  | 0.23 %   invisible
         arm      |  0.97 deg |   108.5 mm  | 1.51 %   faintly visible

       The boom fit is good because BOTH of its pins are surveyed vectors
       (distCylLrgBm1, distCylSmlBm1). The arm fit is worse because only the
       parent pin is a vector (distCylLrgArm); the arm-side pin is the SCALAR
       lenToCylSmlArm = 0.53 and is assumed to sit on the arm centreline. On a
       real machine it sits on a lug ABOVE the arm, and that missing
       perpendicular offset is most of the 108 mm.

       NO BUCKET CYLINDER IS DRAWN. Its pins exist only as scalars
       (lenToCylLrgBkt, lenToCylLrgBkt1/2, lenToCylSmlBkt) with no perpendicular
       component anywhere in the sheet, so there is nothing to aim it at. Adding
       one would mean inventing its geometry.

       Verified that Isaac Sim 6.0 keeps the coefficients: the importer writes
       newton:mimicCoef1 (multiplier) and newton:mimicCoef0 (offset) alongside
       newton:mimicJoint. Tested on this image with a deliberately non-unity
       multiplier before relying on it.

       Coefficients are q -> radians / metres, q being the DRIVING joint in rad:
           barrel pitch  = phi_mult * q + phi_off
           rod extension = d_mult   * q + d_off
       ================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_boom_phi_mult" value="1.330773"/>   <!-- DERIVED  minimax over [-65.73, -27.73] deg -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_boom_phi_off"  value="-0.115918"/>  <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_boom_d_mult"   value="-0.109224"/>  <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_boom_d_off"    value="-0.062094"/>  <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_boom_len_min"  value="1.3341"/>     <!-- DERIVED  shortest pin-to-pin over the range -->
  <!-- ARM-SIDE PIN DIRECTION, SOLVED 2026-09-11.
       The sheet gives only the SCALAR lenToCylSmlArm = 0.53 with no direction, and the
       first pass assumed it lay on the arm centreline. That assumption implies a
       cylinder with len_min 1.9433 m and a stroke of only 0.4415 m, i.e. ret/stroke
       4.40. No cylinder is built like that; the band is roughly 1.4-2.1.
       Sweeping the pin direction and keeping only readings that yield a BUILDABLE
       cylinder picks out a pin ~90 deg off the arm axis, which is also the standard
       layout: the arm cylinder rod pins to a lug standing above the arm near its base.
           alpha = +89 deg : len_min 1.6139, stroke 0.7709, ret/stroke 2.09  <- adopted
           alpha =   0 deg : len_min 1.9433, stroke 0.4415, ret/stroke 4.40  (rejected)
       Two criteria agree, so the direction is DERIVED rather than assumed. What does
       NOT improve is the linear-mimic tracking error: over a 124 deg arm sweep it sits
       at 115-130 mm for every pin direction, because the nonlinearity is inherent to
       the sweep, not to the pin. 130 mm on a 7.2 m machine is 1.8 %. The boom cylinder
       has no such problem (17.7 mm) because its sweep is only 38 deg. -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_arm_pin_alpha" value="89.0"/>       <!-- DERIVED [deg] off the arm axis -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_arm_phi_mult"  value="0.125370"/>   <!-- DERIVED  minimax over [0, 124] deg at alpha = +89 -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_arm_phi_off"   value="0.304604"/>   <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_arm_d_mult"    value="0.356221"/>   <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_arm_d_off"     value="0.077770"/>   <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_arm_len_min"   value="1.6139"/>     <!-- DERIVED -->
  <!-- Barrel outer radius = bore/2 plus a wall; rod radius is the published rod/2. -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_wall"          value="0.010"/>      <!-- ASSUMPTION  m, cosmetic -->
  <xacro:property name="cyl_barrel_frac"   value="0.62"/>       <!-- ASSUMPTION  barrel length as a fraction of len_min -->
  <!-- CYLINDER MASS. The first pass gave these links a 0.5 kg token, reasoning that
       the real mass is already inside the published figures. That was right about the
       accounting and badly wrong about the physics: a 0.5 kg body under the drive
       stiffness the importer applies is numerically unstable, and the arm cylinder
       visibly exploded in the viewer. Mass ratios matter even for cosmetic links.

       So give them realistic masses and CARVE THEM OUT of the links that already
       contain them, which keeps the 9 149.1 kg reconciliation exact:
         arm cylinder  60 kg  <- out of m_boom. Volvo states the 530 kg boom figure
                                 "includes dipper arm cylinder, piping and pin".
         boom cylinder 80 kg  <- out of m_house, which is the residual and therefore
                                 where anything not itemised currently sits.
       Split barrel/rod roughly 70/30, the barrel carrying the tube and both end caps.
       CLASS-TYPICAL: no cylinder weight is published for this machine. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_cyl_boom_total"  value="80.0"/>       <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL  105 mm bore, ~1.33 m -->
  <xacro:property name="m_cyl_arm_total"   value="60.0"/>       <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL   90 mm bore, ~1.61 m -->
  <xacro:property name="m_cyl_boom_barrel" value="${m_cyl_boom_total * 0.70}"/>  <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="m_cyl_boom_rod"    value="${m_cyl_boom_total * 0.30}"/>  <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="m_cyl_arm_barrel"  value="${m_cyl_arm_total * 0.70}"/>   <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="m_cyl_arm_rod"     value="${m_cyl_arm_total * 0.30}"/>   <!-- DERIVED -->

  <xacro:property name="eff_swing"    value="22900.0"/>   <!-- PUBLISHED [L56]  Volvo max swing torque 22.9 kN.m. ECR88D ONLY: the ECR88 Plus publishes no swing torque at all, so do not carry this across. -->
  <xacro:property name="eff_boom"     value="${cyl_relief_pa * pi / 4 * cyl_boom_bore * cyl_boom_bore * 0.4763}"/>
                                  <!-- DERIVED from PUBLISHED [L61]  = 121 256 N.m. 29.4 MPa x pi/4 x (105 mm bore)^2 = 254.6 kN, x the 0.4763 m max moment arm from the surveyed pin geometry. The bore is NO LONGER class-typical: the parts catalogue publishes 105 mm, exactly the value that was assumed, so the +/-10 % bore uncertainty this line used to carry is retired. What remains uncertain is the MOMENT ARM, which rests on the T9 pin-frame assumption. Sanity: holding the front end horizontal at full stretch needs 57.6 kN.m (margin 2.10x). -->
  <xacro:property name="eff_arm"      value="98704.0"/>   <!-- DERIVED from PUBLISHED [L58]  ISO 6015 arm tearout 39.8 kN x (lenArm 1.70 + bucket radius 0.780) = 2.48 m. CONFIRMED 2026-09-11: this implies a 186.2 kN cylinder force, back-solving to an 89.8 mm bore. The parts catalogue publishes 90 mm. Computing it the other way instead (187.0 kN x 0.5300 m) gives 99 124 N.m, 0.4 % away, so the two routes agree and the tearout-derived value is kept because its force is Volvo-published rather than vendor-published. NOTE the 1.70 here is the 1.7 m arm; this value was NOT rescaled for the 2.1 m build. -->
  <xacro:property name="eff_bucket"   value="44616.0"/>   <!-- DERIVED from PUBLISHED [L57]  ISO 6015 bucket breakout 57.2 kN x the 0.780 m digging radius recovered in audit document 5.2. -->
  <xacro:property name="eff_tilt"     value="20000.0"/>   <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL, NO SOURCE [L64]  tiltrotator tilt torque for the 8-12 t class. -->
  <xacro:property name="eff_rotator"  value="10000.0"/>   <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL, NO SOURCE [L64]  tiltrotator rotation torque for the 8-12 t class. -->
  <xacro:property name="eff_inp_link" value="${cyl_relief_pa * pi / 4 * cyl_bkt_bore * cyl_bkt_bore * lenInpLink}"/>
                                  <!-- DERIVED from PUBLISHED [L62]  = 62 076 N.m. CORRECTED 2026-09-11: this was 70 069 N.m, computed from an 85 mm bucket bore taken as CLASS-TYPICAL. The parts catalogue publishes 80 mm, so the old value was 11 % too high. 29.4 MPa x pi/4 x (80 mm)^2 = 147.8 kN, x lenInpLink 0.42 m. -->
  <xacro:property name="eff_conn_rod" value="1.0"/>       <!-- GUESS, TOKEN [L63]  idler and the cut member of the 4 bar. No actuator. Do not read joint torque off this branch. -->
  <xacro:property name="eff_outp_link" value="${eff_bucket}"/> <!-- ALIAS  output_link IS the bucket_joint child, so it is the same joint and the same effort. -->

  <!-- ============================================================================
       [4]  VELOCITY  [rad/s]

       THESE ARE THE MACHINE'S OWN CALIBRATED NUMBERS, not our derivation.
       XpannerLab/X1Exc  ControlModel/Data/ECR88D_LongArm.m, struct reqSpdToActCmd.
       Each axis carries a 3 point table  X = [0, 0.001, vmax]  ->  Y = [0, deadband, cmdmax]
       mapping a REQUESTED JOINT SPEED to a proportional valve command in percent. The
       last X entry is therefore the fastest speed the firmware will ever ask for, and
       the last Y entry is the command it asks for there.

       WHAT THIS REPLACED, AND WHY IT WAS WRONG
       The previous values here were derived as flow / bore area / moment arm with the
       Stage V triple pump (2x68+54 l/min) summed onto one function. Every one of them
       came out 2.0 to 3.0x the firmware number, consistently, which is the signature of
       a wrong assumption rather than scattered error. Two reasons, both checkable:
         - the pump does not sum onto one implement function the way that derivation
           assumed (flagged there as an assumption, [L65]);
         - auto mode caps the valve at PropVlvCmdAutoLimit = 70 % (SysPar.m:251), and
           the tables top out at 60-80 %, so the machine never opens the valve fully.
       For SIL the firmware number is the one that matters: it is the envelope the
       controller actually commands inside. Keeping a limit 2.4x above it only gives an
       overshooting drive room to run away to speeds the real machine cannot reach.

       ASYMMETRY. Every axis is calibrated separately in each direction and they differ
       (boom down is 17 % faster than boom up; link out is 79 % faster than link in).
       URDF carries ONE velocity per joint, so the limit below takes the faster of the
       two and the pair is preserved in section [4b] for the actuator model that needs it.
       ============================================================================ -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_swing"    value="0.5889427"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  swingLe 0.5889 / swingRi 0.5196 rad/s = 5.6 / 5.0 r/min. NOTE this is well under the published free swing speed (8.3 r/min, 0.869 rad/s): swing is the one axis whose command is capped at 60 % instead of 80 %, which is what you would do on a machine carrying glass. The published figure describes the bare ECR88; this one describes it under X1. -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_boom"     value="0.48"/>      <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  bm1Up 0.41 / bm1Down 0.48 rad/s -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_arm"      value="0.43"/>      <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  armIn 0.38 / armOut 0.43 rad/s -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_bucket"   value="0.50"/>      <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  linkIn 0.28 / linkOut 0.50 rad/s. The firmware calls this axis "link", not "bucket": on this machine the cylinder drives the 4 bar input link and the attachment hangs off the output link. Same joint, the firmware's name for it is the more accurate one. -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_tilt"     value="0.2311245"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  tiltPosi 0.2033 / tiltNega 0.2311 rad/s -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_rotator"  value="0.342202"/>  <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  rotPosi 0.3186 / rotNega 0.3422 rad/s = 3.3 r/min. Our CLASS-TYPICAL guess of 9.5 r/min was 2.9x too fast. -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_inp_link" value="${vel_bucket}"/> <!-- ALIAS  the link axis above IS this joint. -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_conn_rod" value="${vel_bucket}"/> <!-- ALIAS  driven branch of the same 4 bar. -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_outp_link" value="${vel_bucket}"/> <!-- ALIAS  same joint as bucket_joint. -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_dozer"    value="0.5"/>       <!-- GUESS  the blade table is a passthrough (X = Y = [0, 0.001, 80]), i.e. the blade is NOT speed calibrated: it is commanded open loop. No firmware speed exists to take. -->

  <!-- ============================================================================
       [4b]  VALVE COMMAND MODEL  [percent]
       Same source. Needed the moment the firmware is in the loop, because the firmware
       does not command joint angles, it commands these valves.

         deadband  the command at which the axis just begins to move (table X = 0.001).
                   Below it the spool has not cracked and the joint does not move at all.
         cmdmax    the command at vmax above.

       Two more from SysPar.m that shape the same signal:
         PropVlvCmdMinOutpHold = 32 % on every axis  (SysPar.m:230-250)
             minimum output once moving, to stop the spool hunting around the deadband
         PropVlvCmdAutoLimit   = 70 %                (SysPar.m:251)
             ceiling in auto mode, below several of the cmdmax values below

       Nothing in the URDF consumes these yet. They live here so the number sits with the
       joint it belongs to when the valve model is written.
       ============================================================================ -->
  <xacro:property name="vlv_swing_le"  value="32.0"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_swing_le_max"  value="60.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_swing_ri"  value="28.7"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_swing_ri_max"  value="60.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_boom_up"   value="34.0"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_boom_up_max"   value="80.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_boom_dn"   value="31.0"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_boom_dn_max"   value="80.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_arm_in"    value="27.0"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_arm_in_max"    value="80.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_arm_out"   value="31.0"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_arm_out_max"   value="80.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_link_in"   value="30.0"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_link_in_max"   value="70.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_link_out"  value="31.0"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_link_out_max"  value="70.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_tilt_pos"  value="19.5"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_tilt_neg"      value="19.5"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_rot_pos"   value="17.5"/>  <xacro:property name="vlv_rot_neg"       value="17.5"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_blade"     value="25.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="vlv_min_hold"  value="32.0"/>  <!-- SysPar.m:230  every axis -->
  <xacro:property name="vlv_auto_max"  value="70.0"/>  <!-- SysPar.m:251  auto mode ceiling -->
  <xacro:property name="ctrl_sample_time" value="0.01"/> <!-- SysPar.m:1  100 Hz, matches the 10 ms CAN3 rate -->

  <!-- ============================================================================
       [5]  JOINT DYNAMICS
       NOT a hydraulic model. Per CLAUDE.md the firmware commands VALVES, not joint
       angles, so the real damping belongs in a per joint cylinder and valve model in the
       SIL harness, not in a URDF scalar. These two exist only so the solver is well
       conditioned. They are deliberately small: under 0.05 % of the main joints' effort,
       and small enough not to freeze the 1 N.m dressing branch.
       ============================================================================ -->
  <xacro:property name="dyn_damping"  value="50.0"/>      <!-- GUESS [L69]  N.m.s/rad, solver hygiene only. -->
  <xacro:property name="dyn_friction" value="10.0"/>      <!-- GUESS [L69]  N.m, solver hygiene only. -->

  <!-- ============================================================================
       [6]  CENTRE OF MASS [m] and INERTIA TENSOR [kg.m^2]
       Optional block: ecr88.urdf.xacro currently recomputes inertia from its placeholder
       primitives, so nothing below is read yet. These are the audit document's section 4
       values, provided so nobody retypes 14 tensors by hand.
       All uniform density primitives aligned with the link frame. Inertia is about the
       link's own CoM. Off diagonals are ZERO: ixy and iyz exactly (every link is modelled
       symmetric about its X-Z plane, all surveyed offsets having y = 0); ixz by
       ASSUMPTION that the link frame axes are the principal axes, which is true by
       construction for an axis aligned box or cylinder but FALSE for the real curved
       gooseneck boom. [L37]
       Triangle inequality ixx+iyy >= izz and its two permutations: CHECKED, all 14 links
       pass. For a uniform box the margin is 2*m*c^2/12 >= 0, so it cannot be violated by
       construction; the composite house_link was checked numerically after the parallel
       axis sum, margins +818, +6 240, +4 439 kg.m^2. [L38]
       ============================================================================ -->
  <!-- base_link: box 2.820 x 2.320 x 0.775 (surveyed undercarriage box; height from
       lenBottomZ1 to lenUppZ1). Tensor is for the FULL 2 713 kg undercarriage: scale by
       m_base_frame/m_base if the tracks are emitted as separate links (H2). -->
  <xacro:property name="com_base_x" value="0.0"/>      <xacro:property name="com_base_y" value="0.0"/>  <xacro:property name="com_base_z" value="-1.0575"/>  <!-- DERIVED, box centroid [L22]. Real CoM is LOWER (tracks, rollers) and slightly FORWARD (blade); uniform density is the reproducible choice and the bias is stated, not tuned away. -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_base_ixx" value="1352.66"/><xacro:property name="inr_base_iyy" value="1933.70"/><xacro:property name="inr_base_izz" value="3014.78"/> <!-- DERIVED [L36] -->
  <!-- house_link: 2 body composite. Counterweight 1 400 kg box 0.50 x 1.90 x 0.75 at
       (-1.040, 0, -0.310), placed from the PUBLISHED 1.29 m tail swing radius and the
       PUBLISHED 760 mm counterweight clearance. House body 3 566 kg box 2.28 x 2.46 x
       1.035 at (+0.200, 0, -0.1525). The house CoM x of -0.150 is fixed FIRST by placing
       the 2.28 m surveyed house box with its rear face on the 1.29 m tail radius; the
       body centroid +0.200 is then BACK SOLVED to reproduce it. One derivation and one
       solve, NOT two agreeing estimates.
       OPEN 200 mm CONFLICT (H4, [L34]): the 2.46 m is the surveyed LenUpY, and Volvo
       publishes the overall upper structure WIDTH as 2 260 mm (Stage V). The survey value
       is kept because section 4 of the audit document is built on the survey throughout.
       If 2.26 m wins, this link is the ONLY consumer and only two diagonals move:
           inr_house_ixx 2 628.40 -> 2 347.87   (house body alone 2 116.7 -> 1 836.1)
           inr_house_izz 5 339.23 -> 5 058.71
           inr_house_iyy 3 528.63 and the CoM are UNCHANGED (neither contains Y). -->
  <xacro:property name="com_house_x" value="-0.150"/>  <xacro:property name="com_house_y" value="0.0"/> <xacro:property name="com_house_z" value="-0.197"/>  <!-- DERIVED [L23][L26] -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_house_ixx" value="2628.40"/><xacro:property name="inr_house_iyy" value="3528.63"/><xacro:property name="inr_house_izz" value="5339.23"/> <!-- DERIVED, composite with parallel axis [L36] -->
  <!-- boom_link: box 3.690 x 0.335 x 1.244, Volvo's PUBLISHED component envelope. The
       1.244 m height is the GOOSENECK CURVATURE envelope, not a filled section, so this
       over states ixx roughly fivefold and iyy by about 10 %. Deliberate: it uses
       published data instead of an invented section depth, and errs high on the axis that
       matters least. -->
  <xacro:property name="com_boom_x" value="1.598"/>    <xacro:property name="com_boom_y" value="0.0"/>  <xacro:property name="com_boom_z" value="0.200"/>  <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL [L28]  0.45 x lenBm1: a curved mono boom is foot heavy (pin bosses, cylinder ears); z is toward the gooseneck. -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_boom_ixx" value="73.31"/>  <xacro:property name="inr_boom_iyy" value="669.73"/><xacro:property name="inr_boom_izz" value="606.33"/> <!-- DERIVED [L36], dims PUBLISHED [L31] -->
  <!-- arm_link: box 2.283 x 0.305 x 0.518, Volvo's PUBLISHED component envelope. -->
  <xacro:property name="com_arm_x" value="0.840"/>     <xacro:property name="com_arm_y" value="0.0"/>   <xacro:property name="com_arm_z" value="0.030"/>  <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL [L29]  0.40 x lenArm = 0.40 x 2.1: boom end pin bosses and cylinder ears dominate. (1.7 m arm: 0.680) -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_arm_ixx" value="10.26"/>   <xacro:property name="inr_arm_iyy" value="188.62"/><xacro:property name="inr_arm_izz" value="183.03"/> <!-- DERIVED [L36], dims PUBLISHED [L32]
       2.1 m dipper, uniform box m=301 kg over the published envelope 2684 x 562 x 305 mm
       (L x H x W): ixx = m/12 (W^2+H^2), iyy = m/12 (L^2+H^2), izz = m/12 (L^2+W^2).
       The same formula reproduces the 1.7 m row (241 kg, 2283 x 518 x 305) as
       7.26 / 110.06 / 106.54, which is what this file carried before the variant switch,
       so the method is unchanged and only the dimensions and mass moved. -->
  <!-- 4 bar members: slender boxes on the surveyed pin to pin lengths. -->
  <xacro:property name="com_gnd_link_x" value="-0.1295"/><xacro:property name="com_gnd_link_y" value="0.0"/><xacro:property name="com_gnd_link_z" value="0.00925"/> <!-- DERIVED, primitive centroid [L30] -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_gnd_link_ixx" value="0.002"/><xacro:property name="inr_gnd_link_iyy" value="0.006"/><xacro:property name="inr_gnd_link_izz" value="0.006"/> <!-- DERIVED, box 0.260 x 0.100 x 0.100 -->
  <xacro:property name="com_inp_link_x" value="0.210"/> <xacro:property name="com_inp_link_y" value="0.0"/><xacro:property name="com_inp_link_z" value="0.0"/> <!-- DERIVED, primitive centroid [L30] -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_inp_link_ixx" value="0.065"/><xacro:property name="inr_inp_link_iyy" value="0.342"/><xacro:property name="inr_inp_link_izz" value="0.370"/> <!-- DERIVED, box 0.420 x 0.160 x 0.100 -->
  <xacro:property name="com_conn_rod_x" value="0.200"/> <xacro:property name="com_conn_rod_y" value="0.0"/><xacro:property name="com_conn_rod_z" value="0.0"/> <!-- DERIVED, primitive centroid [L30] -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_conn_rod_ixx" value="0.035"/><xacro:property name="inr_conn_rod_iyy" value="0.222"/><xacro:property name="inr_conn_rod_izz" value="0.239"/> <!-- DERIVED, box 0.400 x 0.140 x 0.080 -->
  <xacro:property name="com_outp_link_x" value="0.165"/><xacro:property name="com_outp_link_y" value="0.0"/><xacro:property name="com_outp_link_z" value="0.0"/> <!-- DERIVED, primitive centroid [L30] -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_outp_link_ixx" value="0.357"/><xacro:property name="inr_outp_link_iyy" value="0.412"/><xacro:property name="inr_outp_link_izz" value="0.580"/> <!-- DERIVED, box 0.330 x 0.300 x 0.180 -->
  <!-- Tool stack: primitives sized from the surveyed offsets distTiltMntToTilt,
       distRotToAtt, distAttToProbe, distProbeToContactSurface. Sections are CLASS-TYPICAL
       or GUESS [L35]: there is no Xpanner geometry of any kind. -->
  <xacro:property name="com_tilt_mount_x" value="0.140"/><xacro:property name="com_tilt_mount_y" value="0.0"/><xacro:property name="com_tilt_mount_z" value="-0.110"/> <!-- DERIVED, half of distTiltMntToTilt [L30] -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_tilt_mount_ixx" value="0.824"/><xacro:property name="inr_tilt_mount_iyy" value="0.824"/><xacro:property name="inr_tilt_mount_izz" value="0.972"/> <!-- DERIVED, box 0.360 x 0.360 x 0.300 -->
  <xacro:property name="com_tilt_x" value="0.0"/>      <xacro:property name="com_tilt_y" value="0.0"/>  <xacro:property name="com_tilt_z" value="-0.100"/> <!-- DERIVED, primitive centroid [L30] -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_tilt_ixx" value="3.117"/>  <xacro:property name="inr_tilt_iyy" value="2.681"/><xacro:property name="inr_tilt_izz" value="4.148"/> <!-- DERIVED, box 0.450 x 0.500 x 0.300 -->
  <xacro:property name="com_rotator_x" value="0.0"/>   <xacro:property name="com_rotator_y" value="0.0"/><xacro:property name="com_rotator_z" value="-0.13075"/> <!-- DERIVED, half of distRotToAtt [L30] -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_rotator_ixx" value="2.001"/><xacro:property name="inr_rotator_iyy" value="2.001"/><xacro:property name="inr_rotator_izz" value="2.349"/> <!-- DERIVED, cylinder r 0.180, L 0.2615 -->
  <xacro:property name="com_attachment_x" value="0.175"/><xacro:property name="com_attachment_y" value="0.0"/><xacro:property name="com_attachment_z" value="-0.286"/> <!-- DERIVED, half of distAttToProbe [L30] -->
  <xacro:property name="inr_attachment_ixx" value="6.100"/><xacro:property name="inr_attachment_iyy" value="6.100"/><xacro:property name="inr_attachment_izz" value="5.000"/> <!-- DERIVED, box 0.500 x 0.500 x 0.600 -->
